WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.191 --> 00:00:09.840
<v Maya>So I want you to just imagine for a second that you've been reading books your entire life, right? But you're only looking at the overall shapes of whole words.</v>

2
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.181
<v Daniel>Like just the general outline.</v>

3
00:00:11.481 --> 00:00:23.132
<v Maya>Yeah, exactly. Like you instantly recognize the jagged silhouette of the word apple or, you know, the blocky outline of house. Right. But then suddenly someone asks you to spell one of those words out loud or even read it backwards.</v>

4
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:31.683
<v Daniel>You'd be completely lost. I mean, you'd just be guessing, trying to deduce individual letters from this blurry outline that you've just memorized.</v>

5
00:00:32.063 --> 00:00:44.627
<v Maya>Right. And that is exactly how our most advanced artificial intelligence reads today. But for you listening right now, we are taking a deep dive into a really massive shift in how AI processes human language.</v>

6
00:00:45.007 --> 00:00:46.027
<v Daniel>It's a fundamental change.</v>

7
00:00:47.007 --> 00:00:56.095
<v Maya>It really is. Today's exploration is synthesizing this fierce, ongoing academic debate across, I think, six different research papers we have here.</v>

8
00:00:56.195 --> 00:00:56.936
<v Daniel>Yeah, six papers.</v>

9
00:00:57.236 --> 00:01:04.182
<v Maya>But at the center of it all is this brand new, totally paradigm-shifting paper. It was published in Nature on October 7, 2026.</v>

10
00:01:04.602 --> 00:01:05.323
<v Daniel>Hot off the presses.</v>

11
00:01:05.683 --> 00:01:19.952
<v Maya>Literally. It's titled Retrofitting Language Models to Operate Over Bytes by Benjamin Minixhofer and a whole team of researchers. They're from the Allen Institute for AI, Cambridge, the University of Washington, Imperial, and LMU Munich.</v>

12
00:01:20.433 --> 00:01:24.395
<v Daniel>And what they're proposing is this radical new technique. They call it bytefication.</v>

13
00:01:24.975 --> 00:01:27.337
<v Maya>Bytefication. I love that term.</v>

14
00:01:27.591 --> 00:01:36.274
<v Daniel>It's catchy, right? But basically, it's a method that allows these state-of-the-art AI models to finally abandon their 10-year-old, deeply flawed reading habits.</v>

15
00:01:36.814 --> 00:01:43.997
<v Maya>And without forcing developers to spend millions of dollars and massive supercomputer time just retraining them from scratch.</v>

16
00:01:44.388 --> 00:01:50.874
<v Daniel>Exactly. It is essentially teaching these massive neural networks the alphabet while they are already running.</v>

17
00:01:51.035 --> 00:02:05.889
<v Maya>Which is wild. But to truly understand why bitification is even necessary, I think we need to rewind a bit. We need to look at this invisible, highly expensive filter sitting between us and the AI.</v>

18
00:02:06.522 --> 00:02:07.963
<v Daniel>Right. The tokenization trap.</v>

19
00:02:08.003 --> 00:02:17.688
<v Maya>Yes. Let's talk about the tokenization trap, because almost every large language model, what we call an LLM, reads text as subword tokens.</v>

20
00:02:18.048 --> 00:02:23.931
<v Daniel>Yeah. And this mechanism goes way back. It dates back to a paper from 2016 by Senric, Hetto and Birch.</v>

21
00:02:24.512 --> 00:02:24.892
<v Maya>Oh, right.</v>

22
00:02:25.152 --> 00:02:32.576
<v Daniel>They introduced this trick for machine translation called byte pair encoding or BPE. And the core idea there is compression.</v>

23
00:02:33.316 --> 00:02:37.641
<v Maya>So the AI does not see the individual letters like H-E-L-L-O.</v>

24
00:02:37.981 --> 00:02:45.028
<v Daniel>No, not at all. The tokenizer basically scans the text, finds common combinations of letters, and assigns them a numerical ID.</v>

25
00:02:45.749 --> 00:02:46.850
<v Maya>So it's chunking it.</v>

26
00:02:47.311 --> 00:02:55.980
<v Daniel>Exactly. So the AI might see one single token representing the whole chunk, hello, or maybe two tokens representing hell and low.</v>

27
00:02:56.528 --> 00:03:07.636
<v Maya>So the AI is just gulping down text in these prepackaged chunks. And, you know, while that sounds efficient for the machine, the background research we looked at points out two massive costs to doing it this way.</v>

28
00:03:08.176 --> 00:03:08.937
<v Daniel>Very high costs.</v>

29
00:03:09.257 --> 00:03:17.362
<v Maya>Yeah. First, there's a cognitive cost to the AI itself. We have this benchmark study from Edmund, Schmid, and Fraser called CUTE.</v>

30
00:03:17.883 --> 00:03:19.724
<v Daniel>Oh, I love the acronyms in this field.</v>

31
00:03:19.784 --> 00:03:23.927
<v Maya>Right. It stands for Character Level Understanding of Tokens Evaluation.</v>

32
00:03:24.602 --> 00:03:28.792
<v Daniel>And that cute benchmark, it perfectly illustrates the cognitive blind spot we're talking about here.</v>

33
00:03:29.533 --> 00:03:29.854
<v Maya>How so?</v>

34
00:03:30.475 --> 00:03:34.625
<v Daniel>Well, it shows that while these models know how their subword tokens are spelled...</v>

35
00:03:35.169 --> 00:03:37.710
<v Maya>Because they've seen the training data millions of times.</v>

36
00:03:37.911 --> 00:03:48.856
<v Daniel>Exactly. But they possess zero actual ability to manipulate the characters themselves. Like if you ask a state-of-the-art token model to count how many times the letter R appears in a specific word.</v>

37
00:03:49.017 --> 00:03:50.657
<v Maya>Oh, the classic strawberry test.</v>

38
00:03:50.858 --> 00:04:00.983
<v Daniel>Yes. Or if you ask it to simply reverse a string of text, it hallucinates. It stumbles. Because those underlying letters are hidden inside that token wrapper.</v>

39
00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:04.656
<v Maya>It only sees that word-shaped silhouette we talked about earlier.</v>

40
00:04:04.977 --> 00:04:05.437
<v Daniel>Precisely.</v>

41
00:04:05.957 --> 00:04:19.748
<v Maya>But the second cost is where this gets intensely frustrating, I think, because there is a severe financial penalty attached to this system. We have another background paper by Ahiya and colleagues that analyzed the OpenAI API.</v>

42
00:04:20.128 --> 00:04:24.752
<v Daniel>And they looked across 22 typologically diverse languages,</v>

43
00:04:24.872 --> 00:04:30.196
<v Maya>right? Yeah, 22 languages. And it turns out tokenizers are incredibly biased.</v>

44
00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:37.989
<v Daniel>Well, yeah, because they were trained predominantly on English and Latin scripts. So because of that, they're highly optimized to find efficient chunks in English.</v>

45
00:04:38.249 --> 00:04:40.732
<v Maya>Right, but when you move outside that narrow window...</v>

46
00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:42.534
<v Daniel>The system totally falls apart.</v>

47
00:04:42.795 --> 00:04:44.337
<v Maya>They call this over-fragmentation.</v>

48
00:04:44.697 --> 00:04:45.638
<v Daniel>Over-fragmentation.</v>

49
00:04:45.898 --> 00:04:47.080
<v Maya>Okay, so give us an example.</v>

50
00:04:47.458 --> 00:05:02.184
<v Daniel>So if you type a standard sentence in English, the tokenizer might elegantly package it into, say, 10 tokens. OK. But if you input the exact same meaning in Telugu or Amharic, the tokenizer doesn't recognize the patterns. It</v>

51
00:05:02.224 --> 00:05:06.886
<v Daniel>panics, basically, and shreds the text into dozens of tiny, inefficient fragments.</v>

52
00:05:07.507 --> 00:05:13.909
<v Maya>Wow. And since commercial AI vendors charge users per token, the math becomes incredibly unfair.</v>

53
00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:15.070
<v Daniel>It's a huge disparity.</v>

54
00:05:15.485 --> 00:05:23.408
<v Maya>The paper points out that speakers of non-English languages are paying up to five times more money for the exact same amount of information. Five times more.</v>

55
00:05:24.028 --> 00:05:25.569
<v Daniel>And they often get worse results, too.</v>

56
00:05:26.109 --> 00:05:27.209
<v Maya>Wait, really? Why worse?</v>

57
00:05:27.749 --> 00:05:37.873
<v Daniel>Because those extra tokens clog up the model's working memory. It has a limited context window, so taking up more tokens means less actual information fits in.</v>

58
00:05:38.341 --> 00:05:53.333
<v Maya>Oh, that makes total sense. So, okay, if we know tokenization is financially unfair on a global scale and it fundamentally blinds the AI to basic spelling, why hasn't the industry already swapped to feeding these models raw text? Like, why not just use the</v>

59
00:05:53.393 --> 00:05:54.153
<v Maya>underlying bytes?</v>

60
00:05:55.154 --> 00:06:07.404
<v Daniel>And that brings us right to the central diagnosis of the new Nature paper. Byte-level models, meaning models that read raw UTF-8 bytes, they have been the theoretical holy grail for years. Right.</v>

61
00:06:07.642 --> 00:06:14.024
<v Maya>And just to clarify for everyone listening, a UTF-8 byte is basically the fundamental digital building block of text, right?</v>

62
00:06:14.284 --> 00:06:25.287
<v Daniel>Yeah, exactly. A simple Latin letter might be one byte, while a complex character in another language might be three or four bytes. But reading bytes means reading the raw digital DNA of the text.</v>

63
00:06:25.667 --> 00:06:26.827
<v Maya>So why aren't we using them?</v>

64
00:06:27.608 --> 00:06:32.809
<v Daniel>Well, nobody uses them commercially because every time researchers try to build one, they attempt to train it from scratch.</v>

65
00:06:33.429 --> 00:06:35.770
<v Maya>They just throw out everything and start with a blank slate.</v>

66
00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:45.911
<v Daniel>Yes. The problem is that subword token models are a moving target. They are improving at breakneck speed. The data sets are constantly getting refined. The training techniques get better.</v>

67
00:06:46.512 --> 00:06:49.393
<v Maya>So by the time you teach a new model how to read letter by letter...</v>

68
00:06:49.893 --> 00:06:59.399
<v Daniel>Right, which takes an immense amount of computational power and time. By the time that expensive training is finished, the token-based models have already sprinted another mile down the track.</v>

69
00:07:00.079 --> 00:07:00.600
<v Maya>Ah, I see.</v>

70
00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:04.462
<v Daniel>The from-scratch-byte models just cannot catch up to the state of the art.</v>

71
00:07:04.812 --> 00:07:07.533
<v Maya>Which means researchers have basically been fighting a losing battle.</v>

72
00:07:07.753 --> 00:07:08.133
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

73
00:07:08.593 --> 00:07:17.177
<v Maya>So the Nature paper's solution is to stop trying to build a brand new bite-reading car from scratch. Instead, they propose a retrofit.</v>

74
00:07:17.337 --> 00:07:18.377
<v Daniel>Yes, retrofitting.</v>

75
00:07:18.937 --> 00:07:25.520
<v Maya>You take an already brilliant, fully trained subword model and surgically alter it to read raw UTF-8 bytes.</v>

76
00:07:26.151 --> 00:07:36.317
<v Daniel>And the resource efficiency of this retrofitting is just staggering. The authors demonstrate that they can execute this transition using less than 1% of a normal pre-training compute budget.</v>

77
00:07:36.437 --> 00:07:37.758
<v Maya>Wait, less than 1%?</v>

78
00:07:37.838 --> 00:07:42.861
<v Daniel>Less than 1%. They only need to feed it 49.1 billion tokens to make the switch.</v>

79
00:07:43.401 --> 00:07:48.244
<v Maya>That is absolute pennies in the world of frontier AI training. I mean, usually we're talking trillions of tokens.</v>

80
00:07:48.484 --> 00:07:54.385
<v Daniel>Exactly. And they didn't just propose the theory, right? They proved it by building a whole suite of models.</v>

81
00:07:54.786 --> 00:07:55.866
<v Maya>Oh, yeah. Let's walk through those.</v>

82
00:07:56.526 --> 00:08:03.228
<v Daniel>So they created BOMO 7B and BOMO 1B, which they retrofitted from the existing OMO 3 and OMO 2 models.</v>

83
00:08:03.488 --> 00:08:03.768
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

84
00:08:04.088 --> 00:08:10.890
<v Daniel>Then they built BUEN 8B by bitifying QUEN 3 8B. And they made BLAMA 8B from LAMA 3 8B.</v>

85
00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:16.872
<v Maya>So they're taking some of the most capable open source models in the world and teaching them an entirely new way to read.</v>

86
00:08:17.262 --> 00:08:23.149
<v Daniel>Yeah. And just for context, when we say 7B or 8B, we are talking about billions of parameters.</v>

87
00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:24.951
<v Maya>Right. The internal connections.</v>

88
00:08:25.272 --> 00:08:36.265
<v Daniel>Yeah. The synapses of the AI, basically. A 7 billion parameter model is a highly capable system that can actually run on a good laptop, which makes it the perfect proving ground for this technology.</v>

89
00:08:36.867 --> 00:08:40.290
<v Maya>Okay, but I want to push back on the core premise here for a second.</v>

90
00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:40.691
<v Daniel>Sure.</v>

91
00:08:40.831 --> 00:08:55.724
<v Maya>Because a retrofit usually implies a compromise. Like if I rip the transmission out of a classic car and put a modern one in, the chassis is still old, right? Right. So how does slapping a new byte reading interface onto a brain that was originally trained to think in subword</v>

92
00:08:55.764 --> 00:09:01.670
<v Maya>tokens actually make it better? Doesn't that main brain still expect those prepackaged chunks?</v>

93
00:09:02.481 --> 00:09:05.082
<v Daniel>That is the exact engineering hurdle they had to clear.</v>

94
00:09:05.342 --> 00:09:05.623
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

95
00:09:06.283 --> 00:09:14.747
<v Daniel>And to understand how they avoided that compromise, we have to look at the architecture. They designed what they call a latent tokenizer language model pipeline.</v>

96
00:09:15.548 --> 00:09:18.009
<v Maya>Latent tokenizer language model pipeline.</v>

97
00:09:18.189 --> 00:09:18.369
<v Daniel>Yeah.</v>

98
00:09:19.189 --> 00:09:19.930
<v Maya>Sounds very dense.</v>

99
00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:22.971
<v Daniel>It does, but it's actually a highly logical relay race.</v>

100
00:09:23.331 --> 00:09:32.216
<v Maya>Okay, so walk me through the relay. If the raw text comes in as individual bytes, we need something at the front door to sweep them up and organize them. I'm assuming that's the first step.</v>

101
00:09:32.589 --> 00:09:39.902
<v Daniel>Yep, that is the local encoder. So raw bytes stream into this small neural network built using MLSTM layers.</v>

102
00:09:40.483 --> 00:09:41.665
<v Maya>MLSTM.</v>

103
00:09:42.026 --> 00:09:53.161
<v Daniel>Yeah, stands for Matrix Long Short Term Memory. Traditional architectures often really struggle with very long sequences of single letters because they lose track of the context.</v>

104
00:09:53.341 --> 00:09:54.703
<v Maya>That's too much granular detail.</v>

105
00:09:55.003 --> 00:10:08.075
<v Daniel>Exactly. But MLSTM processes data sequentially while using matrix math to keep parallel tracks of memory. So it's incredibly fast and efficient at reading long, continuous streams of raw bytes without losing the plot.</v>

106
00:10:08.775 --> 00:10:18.903
<v Maya>Okay, so the MLSTM reads the individual letters, but the giant pre-trained brain in the middle still expects chunks. So there must be a middleman translating the letters into chunks.</v>

107
00:10:19.363 --> 00:10:19.664
<v Daniel>Yes.</v>

108
00:10:19.924 --> 00:10:20.184
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

109
00:10:20.624 --> 00:10:22.766
<v Daniel>That middleman is called the boundary predictor.</v>

110
00:10:23.186 --> 00:10:24.567
<v Maya>Boundary predictor. Got it.</v>

111
00:10:25.108 --> 00:10:30.272
<v Daniel>Its entire job is to look at the bytes the MLSTM just read and decide where to draw a line.</v>

112
00:10:30.946 --> 00:10:31.867
<v Maya>Like grouping them.</v>

113
00:10:32.167 --> 00:10:41.098
<v Daniel>Exactly. Grouping those bytes into what they call a patch. And once grouped, that patch is mathematically compressed into a single dense representation.</v>

114
00:10:41.258 --> 00:10:41.478
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

115
00:10:41.979 --> 00:10:49.668
<v Daniel>And that representation is what gets handed off to the original massive transformer model, the pre-trained global brain.</v>

116
00:10:50.188 --> 00:10:50.509
<v Maya>I see.</v>

117
00:10:51.010 --> 00:11:02.479
<v Daniel>So the global brain does the heavy complex reasoning on those patches and then hands its deep thoughts to a local decoder, which unpacks the concepts back into individual bytes for the user to read.</v>

118
00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:11.987
<v Maya>Okay. That makes sense. But the paper dwells on a massive obstacle regarding that boundary predictor, right? They call it the look-ahead problem.</v>

119
00:11:12.187 --> 00:11:13.929
<v Daniel>Ah, yes. The look-ahead problem.</v>

120
00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:24.928
<v Maya>Because traditional tokenizers are separate software programs that process the whole document before the AI even turns on. So they have this ultimate unfair advantage. They can peek into the future.</v>

121
00:11:25.168 --> 00:11:37.658
<v Daniel>Exactly. The tokenizers cheat. Like if a traditional tokenizer sees the letters WOR, it can look ahead to see if the next letters are LD, to form world, or maybe ST, to form worst.</v>

122
00:11:38.458 --> 00:11:38.738
<v Maya>Right. And</v>

123
00:11:39.018 --> 00:11:47.002
<v Daniel>it decides how to package the token based on that future knowledge. But a byte model is operating in real time, strictly left to right.</v>

124
00:11:47.522 --> 00:11:52.144
<v Maya>So it sees W-O-R and has to guess right then and there if it should draw a boundary.</v>

125
00:11:52.304 --> 00:11:53.164
<v Daniel>It's flying blind.</v>

126
00:11:53.824 --> 00:11:55.165
<v Maya>So how did the researchers fix that?</v>

127
00:11:55.885 --> 00:12:00.247
<v Daniel>They gave their new boundary predictor exactly one byte of look ahead during processing.</v>

128
00:12:00.347 --> 00:12:00.907
<v Maya>Just one byte?</v>

129
00:12:01.268 --> 00:12:15.336
<v Daniel>Just one. It is allowed to peek exactly one single letter into the future before making its decision. And on the output side, when the model is generating text, they introduced a special boundary symbol, basically an invisible tag.</v>

130
00:12:16.137 --> 00:12:21.486
<v Daniel>This explicitly tells the decoder exactly when a patch is supposed to end, which removes the guesswork entirely.</v>

131
00:12:21.862 --> 00:12:25.244
<v Maya>Wow. That is a remarkably elegant mechanical fix.</v>

132
00:12:25.564 --> 00:12:26.004
<v Daniel>It really is.</v>

133
00:12:26.284 --> 00:12:40.951
<v Maya>But we still have the training problem. Like you have this Frankenstein setup now, right? A brand new MLSTM encoder at the front, a new decoder at the back, and a frozen pre-trained subword brain in the middle. How do you teach them to talk to each other without</v>

134
00:12:41.191 --> 00:12:42.492
<v Maya>breaking the original brain?</v>

135
00:12:43.134 --> 00:12:58.109
<v Daniel>So they engineered a highly specific two-stage training process. In stage one, they put the giant global brain on ice. They freeze its weights so it cannot change or learn anything. They only train the new small bite components</v>

136
00:12:58.129 --> 00:13:11.199
<v Daniel>at the front and back. And the sole objective of stage one is to force the new byte components to perfectly mimic the output of the old token system. So stage one is essentially putting the new eyes into a flight simulator.</v>

137
00:13:11.359 --> 00:13:11.599
<v Maya>Yes.</v>

138
00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:23.909
<v Daniel>You freeze the main engine and you force the new byte readers to practice seeing the world exactly how the old tokenizer used to see it. You make it mimic the old routine perfectly before you trust it to fly the plane.</v>

139
00:13:24.249 --> 00:13:26.611
<v Maya>That flight simulator analogy is spot on.</v>

140
00:13:27.597 --> 00:13:32.122
<v Daniel>And the reason that simulator is absolutely necessary is because of the model's latent space.</v>

141
00:13:32.683 --> 00:13:33.564
<v Maya>The latent space.</v>

142
00:13:34.064 --> 00:13:45.878
<v Daniel>Yeah. It's the abstract multidimensional map where the AI organizes all its concepts. If you just rip out the tokenizer mid-flight and start feeding the brain raw byte signals, that latent space completely collapses.</v>

143
00:13:46.257 --> 00:13:48.298
<v Maya>It just becomes garbage in, garbage out.</v>

144
00:13:48.519 --> 00:13:51.240
<v Daniel>Exactly. And the authors proved this through ablation studies.</v>

145
00:13:51.261 --> 00:13:59.406
<v Maya>Okay, just to clarify for everyone, an ablation study is when researchers test a system by ripping out one specific component to see if the machine crashes, right?</v>

146
00:13:59.506 --> 00:14:07.992
<v Daniel>Yes, exactly. They tried skipping stage one, and the models, especially the smaller one billion parameter ones, they failed spectacularly.</v>

147
00:14:08.032 --> 00:14:08.232
<v Maya>Wow.</v>

148
00:14:08.512 --> 00:14:17.781
<v Daniel>Yeah. The flight simulator stage aligns the new byte readers with the old brain's existing map. And once that alignment is locked in, they move to stage two.</v>

149
00:14:18.201 --> 00:14:19.342
<v Maya>Where they unfreeze everything.</v>

150
00:14:19.542 --> 00:14:29.331
<v Daniel>Right. They open up the global model and train the entire system together end to end. This is where the model stops just mimicking tokens and actually learns how to leverage fine-grained byte level information.</v>

151
00:14:29.592 --> 00:14:40.935
<v Maya>Okay, so we know the mechanics of the flight simulator, but we need to see how the plane actually flies in the real world. Let's look at the scorecard, because the numbers Meenix-Hoffer's team published are pretty striking.</v>

152
00:14:40.995 --> 00:14:41.555
<v Daniel>They really are.</v>

153
00:14:42.195 --> 00:14:47.656
<v Maya>We are looking to see if bitification truly allows these models to catch up to the state-of-the-art token models.</v>

154
00:14:48.096 --> 00:14:50.337
<v Daniel>And the performance retention is undeniable.</v>

155
00:14:50.764 --> 00:15:04.301
<v Maya>Let's examine the 7 billion parameter suite. Buen, the Bitified Quen model, scares a 69.8 on their benchmark suite. Okay. The original token-based QM3 scored 70.3. So 69.8 versus 70.3. That is statistically a tie. It is.</v>

156
00:15:10.074 --> 00:15:18.857
<v Maya>And Bulmo actually outperformed its parent. Bulmo hit 62.5, which edges out the original Ulmo 3's 62.3.</v>

157
00:15:19.097 --> 00:15:24.479
<v Daniel>Which becomes incredibly significant when you compare those numbers to older byte models that were trained from scratch.</v>

158
00:15:25.299 --> 00:15:26.740
<v Maya>Right, the ones that took forever to train.</v>

159
00:15:27.001 --> 00:15:41.887
<v Daniel>Yeah. Those from scratch models like Meta's BLT7B or a model called Evabyte, they are struggling way down in the 40s on these exact same tests. BLT7B scored 48.9. Wow. Evabyte scored</v>

160
00:15:41.947 --> 00:15:43.888
<v Daniel>45.7. So</v>

161
00:15:43.948 --> 00:15:50.550
<v Maya>retrofitting isn't just slightly better than from scratch training. It is a totally different weight class.</v>

162
00:15:50.911 --> 00:15:51.931
<v Daniel>A completely different league.</v>

163
00:15:53.158 --> 00:15:59.881
<v Maya>What about that cognitive cost we discussed earlier, the Qt benchmark, where the token models couldn't spell or reverse words?</v>

164
00:16:00.741 --> 00:16:11.185
<v Daniel>The character understanding score is just skyrocket. For the Quen pair, the Qt score leaps from 66.9 for the token model all the way up to 87.2 for the Bitified version.</v>

165
00:16:11.705 --> 00:16:12.245
<v Maya>Oh my gosh.</v>

166
00:16:12.425 --> 00:16:17.587
<v Daniel>Yeah, from 66 to 87. Yeah, the retrofit gives it a fundamental grasp of the alphabet.</v>

167
